-- title:
--   Endomorphism ring of an isogenous curve as left order
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field, $q'$ a prime with $\mathrm{char}\,\kappa=q'$ and $\kappa$ algebraic over $\mathbb{Z}/q'$, and let $X_1$ be an elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\kappa$ whose affine point group has no $q'$-torsion (every $P$ with $q'\cdot P=0$ is $0$). Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ satisfy $a<0$, $b<0$ and, for each height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, the condition that $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ has all nonzero elements invertible exactly when $q'\in v$; let $\Lambda_1\subseteq\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule which is an order (contains $1$, is multiplicatively closed, spans over $\mathbb{Q}$, finitely generated) and is maximal among such; and let $\theta_1$ be an injective ring homomorphism from the subring of $\mathrm{End}$ of the point group of $X_1$ generated by the rationally represented homomorphisms, with image exactly $\Lambda_1$. Let $x$ be a unit of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$, $W$ an elliptic curve over $\kappa$, $\chi\neq 0$ a rationally represented homomorphism $X_1\to W$, and $d\in\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]^{\times}$ be such that $\theta_1$ maps the kernel ideal $\{\rho\circ\chi:\rho\in\mathrm{Hom}(W,X_1)\text{ rational}\}$ onto the quaternion conjugate $\overline{d\,(\mathbb{H}\cap x\widehat{\Lambda}_1)}$, where $\mathbb{H}\cap x\widehat{\Lambda}_1$ denotes the elements of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ lying in $x$ times the integral adelic box of $\Lambda_1$. Then there is an injective ring homomorphism $\theta_W$ from the rationally generated endomorphism subring of $W$ to $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ whose image is exactly $\mathbb{H}\cap (dx)\widehat{\Lambda}_1 (dx)^{-1}$, $d$ being embedded diagonally, and such that for every rational $\rho\colon W\to X_1$, every $\alpha$ in the endomorphism subring of $W$ and all $e,e'$ in that of $X_1$ with $e=\rho\circ\chi$ and $e'=(\rho\circ\alpha)\circ\chi$ as endomorphisms of the point group, one has $\theta_1 e'=\theta_1 e\cdot\theta_W\alpha$.
--
--   This is Deuring's identification of the endomorphism ring of a curve isogenous to a supersingular curve $X_1$ with the left order of the right ideal attached to the kernel ideal of the isogeny, together with the compatibility condition through $\chi$ that pins the identification down. It supports the passage from isogenies of supersingular curves to ideal classes of quaternionic orders, and is used in the count of automorphisms and level structures on the conjugated order.
